    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cfjkvpJxPwvr"
      },
      "source": [
        "# Digit Recognizer in R: From a Hand-Built Network to a High-Accuracy CNN\n",
        "\n",
        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook tackles Kaggle's [Digit Recognizer](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/digit-recognizer) competition: given a 28 × 28 image of a handwritten digit, predict which digit (0–9) it is.\n",
        "\n",
        "It has two parts:\n",
        "\n",
        "**Part A: Build your own neural network.** Following Stanford's **CS231n** course, we write a 2-layer neural network entirely from scratch with base R matrix operations: forward pass, softmax loss, backpropagation and the Adam optimiser. This shows exactly how a neural network learns, and it reaches about **98%** accuracy.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Part B: Go for maximum accuracy.** Fully connected networks ignore the 2D layout of an image. A **convolutional neural network (CNN)** learns small visual patterns (edges, curves, loops) wherever they appear, which suits images much better. We build one with the `torch` package and add three proven accuracy boosters:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Data augmentation**: randomly shift, rotate and zoom the training digits so the network sees endless new variations\n",
        "- **Batch normalisation and dropout**: steadier training and less overfitting\n",
        "- **Ensembling**: train several CNNs and average their predictions\n",
        "\n",
        "This approach typically scores **about 99.5% or higher**, and it makes the final submission.\n",
        "\n",
        "### The data\n",
        "\n",
        "Each image is flattened into **784 pixel values**, from 0 (background) to 255 (ink).\n",
        "\n",
        "- `train.csv`: 42,000 labelled images (a `label` column plus 784 pixel columns)\n",
        "- `test.csv`: 28,000 unlabelled images we need to predict\n",
        "\n",
        "### Playing by the rules\n",
        "\n",
        "Everything here is trained **only on the competition's `train.csv`**. We don't use the original MNIST dataset that these digits come from, models pretrained on it, or any hand-labelling of the test images. Those would leak the test answers, so they're against the spirit of the competition."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "BbCDa0LIPwvt"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 1. Setup\n",
        "\n",
        "**Turn on the GPU first.** Go to **Runtime → Change runtime type**, make sure **Language** is **R**, choose a GPU (for example **T4 GPU**) under **Hardware accelerator**, and save. The CNN trains many times faster on a GPU. The notebook still works without one, but it automatically trains a smaller ensemble so it finishes in reasonable time.\n",
        "\n",
        "The cell below installs the `torch` package, R's interface to the same engine that powers PyTorch. **The first install takes a few minutes** because it also downloads the torch libraries. If a later cell complains that torch isn't ready, use **Runtime → Restart session** and run this cell again."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "collapsed": true,
        "id": "siatWkWrPwvt",
        "outputId": "27fefd6c-c60b-4282-8716-1d9148c5200e"
      },
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Training device: CPU \n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "options(timeout = 1200)\n",
        "if (!requireNamespace(\"torch\", quietly = TRUE)) install.packages(\"torch\")\n",
        "library(torch)\n",
        "\n",
        "torch_ok <- tryCatch({ torch_tensor(1); TRUE }, error = function(e) FALSE)\n",
        "if (!torch_ok) {\n",
        "  install_torch(reinstall = TRUE)\n",
        "  stop(\"torch finished installing. Now choose Runtime → Restart session, then run this cell again.\")\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "on_gpu <- cuda_is_available()\n",
        "dev <- torch_device(if (on_gpu) \"cuda\" else \"cpu\")\n",
        "cat(\"Training device:\", if (on_gpu) \"GPU\" else \"CPU\", \"\\n\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "QPtaEBRGPwvu"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 2. Download the data from Kaggle\n",
        "\n",
        "We download the competition files with Kaggle's `kagglehub` package. It's a Python package, but Colab has Python installed alongside R, so we call it from R with `system()`.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Before running:**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. On the competition page, click **Join Competition** (or **Late Submission**) and accept the rules. Kaggle blocks downloads until you do.\n",
        "2. Get your credentials **one** of two ways:\n",
        "   - **API token:** on Kaggle, go to **Settings → API → Create New Token**, then paste the token (it starts with `KGAT_`) into the cell below, **or**\n",
        "   - **kaggle.json:** upload your `kaggle.json` file with the folder icon on the left.\n",
        "\n",
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 34
        },
        "id": "x0wryRJaPwvu",
        "outputId": "a4802383-9223-4498-8bbf-bbc69d9b084d"
      },
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/html": [
              "<style>\n",
              ".list-inline {list-style: none; margin:0; padding: 0}\n",
              ".list-inline>li {display: inline-block}\n",
              ".list-inline>li:not(:last-child)::after {content: \"\\00b7\"; padding: 0 .5ex}\n",
              "</style>\n",
              "<ol class=list-inline><li>'sample_submission.csv'</li><li>'test.csv'</li><li>'train.csv'</li></ol>\n"
            ],
            "text/markdown": "1. 'sample_submission.csv'\n2. 'test.csv'\n3. 'train.csv'\n\n\n",
            "text/latex": "\\begin{enumerate*}\n\\item 'sample\\_submission.csv'\n\\item 'test.csv'\n\\item 'train.csv'\n\\end{enumerate*}\n",
            "text/plain": [
              "[1] \"sample_submission.csv\" \"test.csv\"              \"train.csv\"            "
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {}
