      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "# Part B: Convolutional Neural Network for Maximum Accuracy\n",
        "\n",
        "## 8. Turn the data into image tensors\n",
        "\n",
        "A CNN needs the pixels arranged as images rather than flat rows. Torch expects the shape **(images, channels, height, width)**. Our digits are grayscale, so there is 1 channel: `N × 1 × 28 × 28`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Labels become integers from **1 to 10**, because R's torch counts classes from 1. Everything is moved to the GPU if there is one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 34
        },
        "id": "Bw5YApZPPwvy",
        "outputId": "4d6122a6-8a75-4f12-8a38-44c900517c07"
      },
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/html": [
              "<style>\n",
              ".list-inline {list-style: none; margin:0; padding: 0}\n",
              ".list-inline>li {display: inline-block}\n",
              ".list-inline>li:not(:last-child)::after {content: \"\\00b7\"; padding: 0 .5ex}\n",
              "</style>\n",
              "<ol class=list-inline><li>37800</li><li>1</li><li>28</li><li>28</li></ol>\n"
            ],
            "text/markdown": "1. 37800\n2. 1\n3. 28\n4. 28\n\n\n",
            "text/latex": "\\begin{enumerate*}\n\\item 37800\n\\item 1\n\\item 28\n\\item 28\n\\end{enumerate*}\n",
            "text/plain": [
              "[1] 37800     1    28    28"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "to_images <- function(M) {\n",
        "  arr <- aperm(array(M, dim = c(nrow(M), 28, 28)), c(1, 3, 2))   # rows of pixels -> [n, row, col]\n",
        "  torch_tensor(arr, dtype = torch_float())$unsqueeze(2)$to(device = dev)\n",
        "}\n",
        "to_labels <- function(v) torch_tensor(as.integer(v) + 1L, dtype = torch_long())$to(device = dev)\n",
        "\n",
        "x_tr  <- to_images(Xtr);   y_tr  <- to_labels(ytr)\n",
        "x_val <- to_images(Xval)\n",
        "x_all <- to_images(X);     y_all <- to_labels(y)\n",
        "x_te  <- to_images(Xtest)\n",
        "x_tr$shape"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0CIUygp4Pwvy"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 9. Data augmentation\n",
        "\n",
        "People write the same digit slightly shifted, tilted or larger and smaller. **Data augmentation** builds that variety into training: every time a batch is used, each image gets a fresh random **rotation (±10°)**, **zoom (±10%)** and **shift (up to about 2 pixels)**. The network effectively never sees the exact same image twice, which makes it far more robust. This is one of the biggest single accuracy gains on this dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "It is done with an **affine transformation** on the GPU, so it costs almost nothing. Augmentation is used only during training, never on the validation or test images."
      ]
    },
